# Stationery Essentials images look relatively small (learned 2026-09-25)

## Clarification

Owner meant **Stationery Essentials** SKUs looking smaller than other Stickers on the hub — not a generic “random SKUs” note, and not Mixed-only.

## Not the cause

- No Stationery-specific `ProductCard` / image CSS branch (same `imageLayout="full"` → `h-56` + `object-contain` + `inset-1`)
- Not mobile-only styling

## Why they look smaller

Live catalog samples (2026-09-25):

| SKU | Approx pixels | Aspect |
|-----|---------------|--------|
| Happy Bear (Stationery) | 500×500 | 1.0 (square) |
| Just for You / Flower Cat (Stationery) | ~900×1150–1200 | ~0.75–0.80 (portrait) |
| Typical Basic name-label sheets | ~864×1184 | ~0.73 (also portrait) |

So **contain + portrait** alone does not uniquely single out Stationery vs Basic. The usual Stationery “small” read is:

1. **Subject padding in the file** — Stationery Essentials are **file-based stationery** photos (admin free-text size), often with **large empty / white margin** around a smaller product. Name-label sheet mockups usually **fill the canvas**. Same shell + contain → Stationery **subject** looks tiny even when the bitmap is large.
2. **Portrait letterboxing** — tall files still sit as a narrower strip in the landscape `h-56` shell (same class of issue as Mixed Labels).
3. **Low-res square** (e.g. Happy Bear 500×500) can look soft/small on wide laptop cards even though aspect fills better.

## What to do

| Goal | Action |
|------|--------|
| Keep full product visible | Keep contain (do not switch Stationery to cover) |
| Make Stationery look as large as name labels | **Re-upload** tighter crops (less margin, subject fills frame) or nearer-square exports |
| Code-only equal fill | Cover crops — wrong for stickers; avoid |

## Do not

- Add `if (Stationery) scale(1.2)` hacks in `ProductCard`
- Blame missing subcategory CSS before checking the **uploaded file’s subject vs margin**
- Confuse this with Combo Pack description wrapping (`combo-pack-sheet-spec-one-line.mdc`)
